## Managing Ordered Lists with Vec

A Vec keeps items in the order you add them. Combined with a `for` loop, it's the cleanest way to work with ordered collections.

### Building a Friend List

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let friends: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();

  friends.push("Tom");
  friends.push("Lisa");
  friends.push("Aisha");

  for friend in &friends {
    io.println("Friend: " + friend);
  }
  return 0;
}
```

The `for friend in &friends` loop borrows each element, so you can read without moving. Use `&` when you just want to look.

### Adding, Removing, and Checking

```xiom
fn add_friend(friends: &mut Vec[Str], name: Str) {
  friends.push(name);
}

fn remove_friend(friends: &mut Vec[Str], name: Str) {
  let i = 0;
  while i < friends.len() {
    let current = match friends.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    if current == name {
      // Remove by rebuilding without the target
      friends.pop();  // Placeholder: rebuild approach
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
}
```

Actually, removing from the middle of a Vec cleanly requires rebuilding. Let's use a filter approach:

```xiom
fn remove_friend(friends: &mut Vec[Str], name: Str) {
  let kept: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  for f in &friends {
    if f != name {
      kept.push(f);
    }
  }
  // Replace original with filtered list
  friends.pop();  // clear
}
```

Wait — to actually replace, we clear and re-push. A simpler approach: build a new Vec, then swap contents by clearing and re-pushing:

```xiom
fn remove_friend(friends: &mut Vec[Str], name: Str) {
  let kept: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < friends.len() {
    let f = match friends.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    if f != name {
      kept.push(f);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  while friends.len() > 0 {
    friends.pop();
  }
  i = 0;
  while i < kept.len() {
    let item = match kept.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    friends.push(item);
    i = i + 1;
  }
}
```

### Checking Membership

```xiom
fn is_friend(friends: &Vec[Str], name: Str) -> Bool {
  for f in friends {
    if f == name {
      return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

A simple linear scan — works every time for small lists.

